 the building were much  earlier than had been supposed sulfuric  consultant to Essex County Council and  the National authority on medieval  chamber famed buildings saw from the  newly exposed joints that the main hall  had been built in the late 13th century  carpenters marks in Roman numerals  attested to the extensive additions that  came later and reinforced mr. Hewett in  his belief that here was a remarkably  early building much further than the  15th century dating by which it was  officially listed his view was to be  confirmed later by radiocarbon tests on  small samples of oak joists from the  main hall dating the building at about  twelve seventy but further evidence was  to be found Essex County archaeologists  conducted extensive digs concentrating  largely on suspected drainage systems  among many unexpected finds they came up  with a silver penny from the reign of  Henry the third whose rich ran in the  land from 1216 to 1272  with the workmen busy around them the  archaeologists continued their  investigations and unearthed pieces of  cutlery and pottery a medieval ring and  some shells and bones as well as a  lady's shoe  this latter was probably placed with the  silver penny as a good luck talisman  during the original building work but  conclusive proof of the old houses  ancient origins was the discovery of an  open hearth lined with tiles and  surrounded by an oaken curb in the  middle of the main hall this was later  to be restored to its original state and  preserved for display the interest of  Essex County Council archeology section  in this project is to try to elucidate  the building sequence here and also to  see if there are any previous buildings  underneath it we've done other work in  Rockford itself and this will go to help  us build up a pattern of medieval life  in the town from such evidence  historians were indeed able to  reconstruct a picture of life in the  hall with the entire family living  cooking eating and sleeping as well as  often keeping no cattle for neutral work  in the same square chamber open to the  world the harm heaped with logs provided  the main source of warmth light and  cooking facilities with the smoke  drifting upwards to escape through open  louvers in the thatched roof top  the master a man of considerable  substance and his family would probably  have taken their meals at a table on a  raised platform across the north wall  the servants would have eaten at tables  running down the length of the straw  carpet at all and slept on them at night  more radiocarbon tests showed that it  was some twenty or thirty years before  the family could enjoy a little more  privacy in the newly constructed north  wing it was another hundred years before  the servants were provided with a proper  workplace and quarters  with the addition of the south wing in  the case of each wing the only means of  access to the upper floor was by ladder  for the mysterious and still unknown  inhabitants of the old house little  seemed to have changed for the next 400  years in the tiny Market Village at the  lowest Ford over the river Roche life  followed the slow eaters of nature while  in the default vaulted Asian core the  wall of the laser's restful and Henry  the eighth dissolved the monasteries  including the nearby piccolo Priory and  Shakespeare established himself as  England's greatest playwright  but suddenly in South Central  there was a great change in building  styles the old house was caught up in  the great race to modernize among many  other changes an upper floor was  constructed in the main hall with a  dormer window to light it and it was the  addition of this upper floor which  concealed for centuries the fact that  there had indeed been architectural  stirrings at the old house during the  four hundred years sleep as workmen in  the 1980s stripped away the renovations  from down the ages they revealed perhaps  the old houses greatest secret  the Magnificent 16th century chimney in  the main hall that is one of the  buildings greatest attractions today  it's size and the delicate brickwork  typical of the early 15th century are  testimony to the prestige and wealth of  the owner of the old house at that time  only a few chimneys of such elegant  design have been discovered in Essex and  this with its try foiled arches and  diapered patterns is widely regarded as  among the best of them another  remarkable discovery during the 20th  century restoration was the smoke  blackened Timbers in the roof above the  original half evidence of the original  smoke venting system these have been  left untreated  but protected from decay or insect  attacked by the heavy suit incrustation  the 17th century burst a building added  a chimney stack of less impressive but  still cherishable design in the north  wing as well as staircases to the upper  floors and an extension behind the main  hall this work extending into the 18th  century marks the heyday of the old  house as a home for a family of some  prestige from then on the building  slipped gradually into a long slow  decline which continued until Rochford  council decided to restore it  the old house was being used as a  Chandler's shop by George Garrett in the  18th century when asked where Georgian  Bay was added to the front of the north  wing in the early 19th century George  Turner a cobbler rented the old house as  premises from Thomas Hearst Marsh who  also owned the neighboring red house  during the last half of that century  John Hitchcock another cobbler could be  seen making boots and shoes in one  window while his wife Emma an  accomplished NIDA woman sat in the other  running their fancy goods business the  old house passed through several hands  subsequently being used as a family home  by among others a solicitor and a  retired minister when Rochford counsel  acquired it for 45,000 pounds in January  1982 many believe the old house had long  past any kind of useful life certainly  