 new man-made beam even more strength  and resilience than the original work  while at the same time leaving its  outward appearance unchanged similar  techniques were used all over the old  house weakened beams were boxed in with  plywood and epoxy resin poured in to  give renewed strength after sanding and  staining they were indistinguishable  from the original replacement of minor  members such as these stud fixings  brought into play epoxy bonded dowel  joints to replace the traditional  mortise and Tenon's new sections of  posts and beams were spliced in with  seat cuts spliced joints or traditional  scarf joints gentle sandblasting cleaned  and brightened all the framework without  damaging the original tool markings and  timber preservative was applied to guard  against further infestation and rot an  exciting discovery during the work was  the location of diamond-shaped window  mullions destroyed long ago and new  timbers were inserted to recall the  original effect  these windows would have been unglazed  and covered by wooden shutters at night  rotted insect eaten and broken rafters  were built up with the aid of softwood  top plates giving renewed strength and  straightening the new roofline thin flat  steel bracing gave the roof a lateral  stability that was lacking in the  original construction the old joints of  repaired rafters came back together  neatly allowing the new oak pegs to  slide sweetly into the original dowel  holes wherever possible in the  restoration spare wood from  reconstruction was cleaned up and used  in other parts of the house but much of  the timber brought in was in fact far  from new a source of well seasoned oak  was found in the demolition of an  overhead country a John SADS old timber  wharf at Molden just a few miles up the  Essex cursed this was fashioned and cut  to size by barons of West Cliff who wore  out many a slow blade on the iron like  timber despite the necessary  introduction of modern methods the  decision to use traditional materials  and techniques as far as possible was  rigorously applied even to the reclad  enough the walls modern fiberglass quilt  insulation vapor barriers and breather  papers were allied to old methods with  larvan plaster to create an effective  but traditionally based skin for the old  house one inch by half inch lads secured  at half inch intervals to the stubs were  coated in traditional lime hair plaster  and instead of using a modern external  emulsion the old values of lime wash  were revived it was made on-site to the  age-old formula with lime putty and  tallow heated and blended in a large  tank the distemper for the inside walls  set off a nationwide search for a  manufacturer of this long ignored  coating the last remaining maker was  tracked down in the Irish Republic  when the old house was first built it  had a fetched roof this was later  replaced by burnt clay tiles many had  survived the stripping down and were  reused as the roof was made  weather-tight  above its modern insulation one of the  last stages in the long process of  restoration was the replacement of the  floors one room alone needed little  extra work  the upstairs back room of the north wing  had its original oak floor board still  in good condition and secured with wide  blacksmith's nails  apart from cleaning it needed little  restoration testimony to the quality of  work put into the Masters bedroom  elsewhere wooden floors were covered  with plywood and strengthened to conform  with modern office use specifications  ground floors were concreted over damped  roof membranes and paved with brick the  biggest headache of all was the  provision of all the services needed to  run a modern office heating water power  computer cables telephone links and  security systems all had to be concealed  or at least disguised to maintain the  atmosphere of antiquity inside and out  this was achieved to a remarkable degree  aided by careful and discreet choices of  furniture carpets and curtains which  balanced modern efficiency with a  lasting impression of age in keeping  with the spirit of the whole project  even the fine oak refectory table and  matching chairs in the main hall were  made from wood leftover from repairs to  the timber frame in all the restoration  of the old house to its early splendor  took 10 months and cost 235 thousand  pounds was it all worth it certainly it  was if only as an example of how our  towns early history can be thrown into  sharp relief for future generations by  dedicated and conscientious attention to  minut detail  the old house has now been designated a  grade-two starred listed building an  award previously withheld because the  house was in such a poor state of repair  there was no doubt either in the minds  of the town's folk who crowded into the  old house at its official opening by  Councillor miss Beryl Lovett chairman of  the Council on the 8th of December 1983  and a lot of you here have children and  their children and their children's  children will be able to come and look  at this house and they'll learn from the  house itself the history of the place in  which they live and I think that's  really very splendid I think we are very  fortunate to have such a place in our  area and I must say it looks beautiful  beyond all my wildest dreams and I want  to say how very proud I am to be part of  a council that got on with it and made  sure that it stayed here rather than let  let it just fall to the ground because  that's what would have happened  I'm finding it is little strange that  I'm supposed to open 17 South Street I  think that she never closed and she just  had a little rest with pleasure I pull  this cord and I reopen centium South  Street and god bless all who work in her  in the run-up to Christmas more than  3,000 people came to admire the amazing  transformation from ruin to pride and  still they come the young and the not  sir young on frequent guided tours while  the essential work of the council goes  on around them against all the odds the  700 year old house is alive again and  ready for another 700 years  but knit the last words be with  counseling miss lammott and the chief  executive of the cook at that memorable  opening ceremony the house ladies and  gentlemen is yours for only don't take  it away and I'm sure they never will

